Expressions, automata, and the neighbourhood system of formal languages.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`concat`).** The concatenation of two languages consists of all words obtained by appending a word of the second language to a word of the first.

**Definition (`InS`).** The admissible regular languages are generated by the universal language and singleton languages, and are closed under concatenation and nonempty intersection.

**Theorem (`mem_concat`).** The element described by concat belongs to the set or neighbourhood specified by its hypotheses.

*Proof.* The required witness for concat is supplied directly by the defining membership condition. ∎

**Theorem (`Ssys_master_mem`).** The master neighbourhood in ssys master mem is the universal set and satisfies the claimed relation.

*Proof.* Following the defining recursion for ssys master mem and applying the preceding component lemmas yields the claimed result. ∎

**Theorem (`Ssys_master_nonempty`).** The set or language ssys master nonempty has an element under the hypotheses of the theorem.

*Proof.* Choose witnesses from the nonempty input sets in ssys master nonempty and combine them using the defining construction. ∎

**Theorem (`Ssys_sub_master`).** The master neighbourhood in ssys sub master is the universal set and satisfies the claimed relation.

*Proof.* Following the defining recursion for ssys sub master and applying the preceding component lemmas yields the claimed result. ∎

**Theorem (`concat_mono`).** Enlarging the input sets in concat mono can only enlarge its resulting set or element.

*Proof.* Transport every witness for concat mono along the assumed inclusions; the same witness then belongs to the enlarged result. ∎

**Theorem (`concat_nonempty`).** The set or language concat nonempty has an element under the hypotheses of the theorem.

*Proof.* Choose witnesses from the nonempty input sets in concat nonempty and combine them using the defining construction. ∎

**Theorem (`InS.nonempty`).** The set or language nonempty has an element under the hypotheses of the theorem.

*Proof.* Choose witnesses from the nonempty input sets in nonempty and combine them using the defining construction. ∎

**Theorem (`Ssys_inter_mem`).** The construction ssys inter mem preserves the specified intersection of sets or neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Use the same witnesses in both directions for ssys inter mem, combining their inclusion proofs pointwise and using nonemptiness where intersection closure requires it. ∎

**Definition (`Ssys`).** The regular-language neighbourhood system has all Boolean words as master set and the nonempty languages generated from the universal language and singleton languages by concatenation and intersection as neighbourhoods.

**Theorem (`Ssys_mem`).** For the objects in ssys mem, the named equality, inclusion, or finite-witness relation holds.

*Proof.* Following the defining recursion for ssys mem and applying the preceding component lemmas yields the claimed result. ∎

**Definition (`mulElem`).** Multiplication of language elements is induced by concatenating every neighbourhood from the first element with every neighbourhood from the second.

**Theorem (`mem_mulElem`).** The element described by mul elem belongs to the set or neighbourhood specified by its hypotheses.

*Proof.* The required witness for mul elem is supplied directly by the defining membership condition. ∎

**Definition (`emb`).** A word is embedded as the principal language element generated by its singleton language.

**Theorem (`mem_emb`).** The element described by emb belongs to the set or neighbourhood specified by its hypotheses.

*Proof.* The required witness for emb is supplied directly by the defining membership condition. ∎

**Definition (`prependMap`).** Prefixing by a fixed word defines an approximable endomap of the regular-language neighbourhood system.

**Definition (`repeatWord`).** The nth repetition of a word is obtained by concatenating n copies, with the zeroth repetition equal to the empty word.

**Theorem (`repeatWord_zero`).** At input zero, repeat word has the initial value specified by its definition.

*Proof.* The relevant clause of the recursive definition of repeat word zero gives this equation immediately. ∎

**Definition (`powerLang`).** The power language of a word is the set of all of its finite repetitions.

**Theorem (`mem_powerLang`).** The element described by power lang belongs to the set or neighbourhood specified by its hypotheses.

*Proof.* The required witness for power lang is supplied directly by the defining membership condition. ∎

**Definition (`streamArrow`).** The stream arrow of a word is the least fixed point of prefixing by that word.

**Definition (`streamElemMem`).** A language neighbourhood belongs to the stream element of a word when it contains a sufficiently long finite power of that word.

**Definition (`streamElem`).** The stream element generated by a word consists of all admissible languages containing some finite power of that word.

